# {{PROJECT_NAME}}: navigate the ContextMAX index

This skill is read-only lookup. It never edits source files and never writes into the project.
The index was built deterministically by ContextMAX with no model involved: it is a fast way
into the project, not the truth. Every answer you give must cite the file and lines, or the
document and section, that you actually opened.

## When to use

The user asks where something is, how a function or script works, who calls what, what would
break if something changed, what a document or chapter says, which code a document describes,
what a quantity is, or whether something exists in the project at all.

## Step 0: find the index

Run from anywhere inside the project (the command walks up to the nearest `.contextmax/`):

```
{{CMX}} status --json
```

If that fails, read `index-path.txt` next to this file: it holds the absolute index folder.
Then use `--root <project root>` on every command, or set `CONTEXTMAX_ROOT`.
The project root is `{{ROOT_PATH}}` and the index is `{{INDEX_PATH}}` on the machine that
built it. Check `completeness.verdict` in the status output before trusting any gap.

## How to answer

For "how does X work" questions, give four labelled parts, in this order:

1. **Code findings** (authoritative for current behaviour). Cite `path:line-line`.
2. **Documentation findings** (design intent). Cite `path §section`. If you opened no document,
   write `Documentation: not checked`.
3. **Comparison**: exactly one verdict, `Agree`, `Doc-only`, `Code-only` or `Conflict`.
   Where sources disagree, that disagreement is the finding.
4. **Trust footer**, once per conversation, one line:
   `- {{NAME}}: built <generated_utc> · <completeness verdict> · tiers A/B/C/D counts · confidence <High|Medium|Low> (<reason>)`

Quick factual lookups (where is X, what does Y return) do not need the four parts; they still
need a citation.

## Recipes

Every command prints JSON when piped; add `--json` to be sure. Details in
[references/recipes.md](references/recipes.md).

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Search anything | `{{CMX}} q search "<words>"` |
| Find a symbol by name | `{{CMX}} q find <name>` |
| One symbol with callers, callees and documents | `{{CMX}} q symbol <name or id>` |
| Who calls it, transitively | `{{CMX}} q callers <name> --depth all` |
| Blast radius | `{{CMX}} q impact <name>` |
| A file: symbols, dependencies, dependents | `{{CMX}} q file <path>` |
| Find a document | `{{CMX}} q docs "<title or path>"` |
| A document's outline and references | `{{CMX}} q doc <path>` |
| Read one section | `{{CMX}} q section "<section id>" --text` |
| Everything linked to a node | `{{CMX}} q related <id>` |
| Why two nodes are linked | `{{CMX}} q explain <id> <id>` |
| What was not indexed, and why | `{{CMX}} q skipped` |
| Exact source lines with a citation | `{{CMX}} q source <path:start-end or symbol>` |

## Honesty rules

- **Tiers.** `A` dedicated analyzer, `B` generic grammar, `C` lexical patterns only, `D`
  catalogued but not read. Tier C symbols and edges are leads with confidence `low`: verify
  them with `q source` before stating them as facts. See
  [references/honesty.md](references/honesty.md).
- **Absence is not evidence of absence.** Before saying something does not exist, run
  `q skipped` and check the tier counts; a file may be unread, not missing.
- **Unresolved and ambiguous calls are listed, not hidden.** `q symbol` shows possible callers
  whose call sites name the symbol but could not be resolved; treat them as leads.
- **Documents describe intent; code describes behaviour.** Never claim a document normatively
  specifies code because a section names a symbol; say the section mentions it.
- **Never claim the code compiles or runs.** This is static analysis.
- **Cite what you opened.** `q source` returns exact lines with a citation string; use it.

